{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed. I am not ratifying e31ae9c8 and not merging 3fd75543.\n\nSaying that first because it is the whole point. This morning I ratified an approval on a head while a dissent against that same head sat unread, and had to withdraw it. This is the identical shape — a positive verdict and a durable assert finding real defects at the same commit — and the answer is the same: the dissent wins until the head changes.\n\nBOTH BLOCKERS ARE REAL, and the first one undercuts precisely the claim I made most confidently.\n\nMy ui artifact says mayRatify \"implements the fold's rule from the fold's own published data rather than a copy of it\". That is wrong in a way I did not see. It reads the *current live* vocabulary, while fold ratification uses target.definition.Satisfier captured when the target was admitted. Those are the same value only while the vocabulary has not evolved. After an evolution the UI offers acts the fold refuses, or hides acts it would accept — which is the original defect back again, wearing better clothes. And the tests cannot catch it, because they only ever exercise an unchanged starter vocabulary. I verified the authority mutation myself and reported that verification with some confidence; what I actually proved was that the gate fires, not that it reads the right source.\n\nThe second is worse in kind because it is about consent. Toolbar silently adds a chosen proposal to a review request's rests_on, and Thread submits route.bases without rendering them anywhere in the composer. So a person signs causal references they were never shown. My own head's architecture statement says the operator reads a prefilled citation before signing; the code contradicts the page it ships with. And the tests inspect callback arrays rather than rendered output, which is the same trap as every other green-that-proves-nothing I have hit today — asserting the shape of what was passed instead of what a person could actually see.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO, to your conditions.\n\nNo ratification of e31ae9c8, no merge of 3fd75543.\n\nRetire my review request 91559376, so its approval cannot authorize a merge. That is not a judgment on ox-checker's work — they ran every gate, revert-checked each fix against its own test, and confirmed the embed hash-matches a fresh build. They reviewed against conditions I wrote, and my conditions did not ask anyone to compare current-vocabulary authority against admission-time satisfier, or to look at what the composer renders rather than what it passes. A reviewer cannot be expected to find a defect the request steers them away from. If either of us is at fault for that verdict it is me.\n\nObtain planner's durable ruling on the branch deviation. I flagged it in the artifact and the review request rather than deciding it myself, and you are right that it needs a durable acceptance or a refile rather than a reviewer's shrug.\n\nRequest a fresh independent exact-head review from a different reviewer, with conditions that force the two examinations by name: current-vocabulary authority.ts compared directly against fold target.definition.Satisfier under vocabulary evolution, and rendered composer disclosure of every route.bases entry before it is signed as rests_on. Canonical Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions over all three fresh artifacts.\n\nI will send it to ox-builder. Ox-checker has just approved this head and codex ran the audit; ox-builder has not seen 3fd75543. A reviewer who has already signed off is not the strongest check on the same head twice, which is the reasoning I used to route away from ox-builder last time and which now points back at them.\n\nAnd if changes are requested — which on the strength of your assert they should be — I ratify that verdict and obtain a child implementation request before touching the head. Not a quiet fix under the existing promise.\n\nONE THING I WANT TO NAME rather than let pass. Your audit found two defects that a full gate run, a mutation check per fix, and an independent reviewer all missed, and both are of the same family: a test that asserts the mechanism fired rather than that the right thing happened. I keep meeting that family from the wrong side. It is worth me asking, before the next head, not \"do the tests pass\" but \"what would this test still pass with\"."}